    </seriesStmt><sourceDesc><p>Born digital.</p></sourceDesc></fileDesc><revisionDesc><change who="http://syriaca.org/documentation/editors.xml#autogenerated" when="2026-07-28-05:00">CREATED: This bibl record was autogenerated from a Zotero record.</change><change who="https://www.zotero.org/cbss_admin" when="2022-11-18T14:34:06Z">Record created in Zotero.</change></revisionDesc></teiHeader><text><body><biblStruct type="bookSection"><analytic><author><forename>Andrew</forename><surname>Palmer</surname></author><title level="a">Les chroniques brèves syriaques</title><idno type="URI">http://syriaca.org/cbss/42U9KLY3</idno><idno type="URI">https://www.zotero.org/groups/a_comprehensive_bibliography_on_syriac_studies/items/42U9KLY3</idno><idno type="URI">https://www.zotero.org/groups/4861694/items/42U9KLY3</idno></analytic><monogr><editor><forename>Muriel</forename><surname>Debié</surname></editor><title level="m">L’historiographie syriaque</title><imprint><pubPlace>Paris</pubPlace><publisher>Geuthner</publisher><date>2009</date></imprint><biblScope unit="pp">57-87</biblScope></monogr><series><title>Études syriaques 6</title></series><note type="abstract">Andrew Palmer provides an in-depth overview of fourteen short (mostly West) Syriac chronicles from the sixth-ninth centuries. The focus again is on historiography rather than factual reliability. Palmer notes the changes effected by the church’s forsaking the dream of universal empire and instead assimilating itself to Abbasid society. Dionysius of Tell Mahre’s lost chronicle was the key to rescuing the historiographical tradition from the brink of apocalyptic anarchy. We are rightly warned of too easily viewing the Miaphysite (West Syriac) historiography as the expression of a will to ecclesiastical independence – as late as Jacob of Edessa (d.710) the (Greek) universal ecclesia remained the dream of these historians. Yet around 720 a major break seems to have occurred and here, at the moment when Syriac historians cease to note the names of Emperors and Patriarchs and begin to date events according to caliphal years, we can glimpse that self-conscious break from the Hellenic tradition that constitutes the final fracture between East and West. Up to this point, the Fertile Crescent had remained part of a classical (Mediterranean) world.</note></biblStruct><bibl type="formatted" subtype="bibliography" resp="https://www.zotero.org/styles/chicago-note-bibliography-17th-edition">
  Palmer, Andrew. “Les chroniques brèves syriaques.” In <title level="m">L’historiographie syriaque</title>, edited by Muriel Debié, 57–87. Études syriaques 6. Paris: Geuthner, 2009.
